/*
  core/stressors/repeated_failure.spthy -- f_U for sigma10 RepeatedFailure.

  CHAINING detector. Unlike the other migrated detectors, this one does NOT read the Layer-1 !Step
  interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md) -- and correctly so. Its trigger is not a step the
  CEREMONY presents to the user, but a FAILURE OUTCOME the human layer itself produced: !Failed(P) is
  emitted by core/masks/busy_op.spthy when a Busy user slips. So sigma10 reads a downstream signal of
  another stressor (e.g. inferred sigma1 slip -> !Failed -> sigma10), which is why it is the model's
  first chaining edge.

  This is already ceremony-agnostic: !Failed(P) carries no task name and is produced by the core mask
  layer, so a new ceremony incurs no vocabulary burden -- the marker arises automatically whenever a
  slip occurs. Converting it to !Step would be wrong: a failure is not a presented interaction step.
  (NASA-TLX Frustration is the human-factors anchor; the observable is the failure event, not a
  per-step demand, so there is no lexicon row.)

  At onset emits SetMask(P,'Careless') -- escalation to disengagement. Reads the persistent !Failed
  read-only (no reproduce). OneRepeatedFailure bounds it once/party (else it re-fires on the
  persistent !Failed and blows up mask_state.spthy's interval reasoning).
*/

rule Detect_RepeatedFailure:
    [ !Failed(P), !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ RepeatedFailure(P), SetMask(P,'Careless') ]->
    [ ]

restriction OneRepeatedFailure:
  "All p #i #j. RepeatedFailure(p)@i & RepeatedFailure(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
